x86/tdx: take a guest's private pages out of the kernel direct map

SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only

Written against Ubuntu linux 7.0.0-43.43 (resolute), which is upstream v7.0
plus Ubuntu's changes. Apply with "patch -p1" in the top of that tree.

A page that TDH.MEM.PAGE.ADD or TDH.MEM.PAGE.AUG has given to a TDX guest is
still mapped writable in the host kernel's direct map. A host store through
that mapping lands with the host's key and clears the TD-owner bit of the
64-byte line. The guest's next read of the line is a machine check.

With this patch the host kernel has no mapping of such a page while a guest
owns it:

  - guest_memfd splits the direct map around the page to 4K before the page
    is handed to KVM. The split can allocate and sleep, so it is done there
    and not under mmu_lock.
  - After ADD or AUG returns success, the page's cache lines are flushed once
    more, its 4K direct-map entry is cleared, and the kernel TLB is flushed
    on every CPU. The flush has to come first: CLFLUSHOPT faults on a page
    that is not mapped. A failed SEAMCALL leaves the page mapped.
  - Secure EPT pages, TD control pages and the ADD source page stay mapped.
  - When the page is removed from the guest, the entry is put back after
    TDH.PHYMEM.PAGE.WBINVD succeeds and before tdx_quirk_reset_page(), which
    writes through the direct map.
  - /proc/tdx_direct_map counts each change: hide_ok, hide_fail,
    unshield_ok, unshield_fail.

KVM is a module, so set_memory_4k(), set_direct_map_invalid_noflush(),
set_direct_map_default_noflush() and flush_tlb_kernel_range() are exported.

